# 🚨 Error Handling

## Core Principles
- **Fail gracefully**: Never show raw error messages or stack traces to users
- **Provide context**: Users should understand what went wrong and what to do
- **Log everything**: Errors should be logged with context for debugging
- **Recover when possible**: Offer fallbacks and recovery options
- **Don't swallow errors**: Always handle or propagate errors

## Error Boundaries (React)

### Always Use Error Boundaries
- Wrap route segments with error boundaries
- Provide fallback UI with recovery options
- Log errors to monitoring service

```tsx
// ✅ Good - error.tsx in Next.js App Router
'use client';

export default function Error({
  error,
  reset,
}: {
  error: Error & { digest?: string };
  reset: () => void;
}) {
  useEffect(() => {
    // Log to error monitoring service
    console.error('Error boundary caught:', error);
  }, [error]);

  return (
    <div className="error-container">
      <h2>Something went wrong</h2>
      <p>We're sorry for the inconvenience. Please try again.</p>
      <button onClick={reset}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Granular Error Boundaries
- Don't just wrap the root—wrap individual features
- Allow parts of the page to fail without crashing everything
- Provide component-level error boundaries for critical sections

```tsx
// ✅ Good - component-level boundary
function UserDashboard() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Header /> {/* If this fails, rest of page still works */}
      <ErrorBoundary fallback={<FeedError />}>
        <UserFeed /> {/* Isolated error boundary */}
      </ErrorBoundary>
      <ErrorBoundary fallback={<SidebarError />}>
        <Sidebar />
      </ErrorBoundary>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Async Error Handling

### Always Use Try-Catch
```tsx
// ✅ Good - proper error handling
async function fetchUser(id: string) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Failed to fetch user: ${response.statusText}`);
    }
    
    const data = await response.json();
    return data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching user:', error);
    // Re-throw or return error state
    throw new Error('Unable to load user data. Please try again.');
  }
}

// ❌ Bad - no error handling
async function fetchUser(id: string) {
  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  return response.json();
}
```

### Server Actions (Next.js)
```tsx
// ✅ Good - typed error responses
'use server';

export async function createUser(data: FormData) {
  try {
    const email = data.get('email') as string;
    
    // Validation
    if (!email) {
      return { success: false, error: 'Email is required' };
    }
    
    // Business logic
    const user = await db.user.create({ data: { email } });
    
    revalidatePath('/users');
    return { success: true, data: user };
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to create user:', error);
    return { 
      success: false, 
      error: 'Failed to create user. Please try again.' 
    };
  }
}
```

## API Error Handling

### Standardized Error Responses
```tsx
// ✅ Good - consistent error format
export async function GET(request: Request) {
  try {
    const data = await fetchData();
    return Response.json({ success: true, data });
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('API Error:', error);
    
    return Response.json(
      { 
        success: false, 
        error: 'Failed to fetch data',
        code: 'FETCH_ERROR'
      },
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}
```

### HTTP Status Codes
Use appropriate status codes:
- `200` - Success
- `201` - Created
- `400` - Bad Request (client error)
- `401` - Unauthorized
- `403` - Forbidden
- `404` - Not Found
- `422` - Unprocessable Entity (validation error)
- `500` - Internal Server Error
- `503` - Service Unavailable

### Validation Errors
```tsx
// ✅ Good - detailed validation errors
import { z } from 'zod';

const userSchema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email('Invalid email format'),
  age: z.number().min(18, 'Must be 18 or older'),
});

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  try {
    const body = await request.json();
    const validated = userSchema.parse(body);
    
    // Process validated data
    return Response.json({ success: true });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof z.ZodError) {
      return Response.json(
        { 
          success: false, 
          error: 'Validation failed',
          details: error.errors 
        },
        { status: 422 }
      );
    }
    
    return Response.json(
      { success: false, error: 'Internal error' },
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}
```

## User-Facing Error Messages

### Clear, Actionable Messages
```tsx
// ✅ Good - helpful message with action
"Unable to save your changes. Please check your connection and try again."

// ❌ Bad - technical jargon
"POST /api/users failed with status 500"

// ❌ Bad - no context
"Error"
```

### Error Message Patterns
- **Network errors**: "Unable to connect. Please check your internet connection."
- **Validation errors**: "Email is required" or "Password must be at least 8 characters"
- **Permission errors**: "You don't have permission to access this resource."
- **Not found**: "We couldn't find that page. It may have been moved or deleted."
- **Server errors**: "Something went wrong on our end. We're working to fix it."

### Toast Notifications
```tsx
// ✅ Good - contextual toast
import { toast } from 'sonner';

try {
  await saveUser(data);
  toast.success('Profile updated successfully');
} catch (error) {
  toast.error('Failed to update profile. Please try again.');
  console.error('Save error:', error);
}
```

## Form Error Handling

### Field-Level Validation
```tsx
// ✅ Good - show errors next to fields
<div>
  <label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
  <input
    id="email"
    type="email"
    aria-invalid={!!errors.email}
    aria-describedby={errors.email ? 'email-error' : undefined}
  />
  {errors.email && (
    <p id="email-error" className="error-text">
      {errors.email}
    </p>
  )}
</div>
```

### Form-Level Errors
```tsx
// ✅ Good - summary of form errors
{formError && (
  <div role="alert" className="form-error">
    <strong>Unable to submit form:</strong>
    <ul>
      {Object.entries(errors).map(([field, message]) => (
        <li key={field}>{message}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </div>
)}
```

## Loading and Error States

### Always Handle All States
```tsx
// ✅ Good - all states handled
function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const { data, error, isLoading } = useUser(userId);
  
  if (isLoading) return <Skeleton />;
  if (error) return <ErrorState error={error} retry={() => refetch()} />;
  if (!data) return <NotFound />;
  
  return <ProfileCard user={data} />;
}

// ❌ Bad - no error state
function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const { data } = useUser(userId);
  return <ProfileCard user={data} />; // crashes if data is undefined
}
```

## Graceful Degradation

### Fallback Content
```tsx
// ✅ Good - fallback when feature fails
function UserAvatar({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const [error, setError] = useState(false);
  
  if (error) {
    // Fallback to initials
    return <InitialsAvatar userId={userId} />;
  }
  
  return (
    <img
      src={`/avatars/${userId}`}
      alt="User avatar"
      onError={() => setError(true)}
    />
  );
}
```

### Optional Features
```tsx
// ✅ Good - degrade gracefully if analytics fails
try {
  trackEvent('page_view', { page: '/dashboard' });
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Analytics error:', error);
  // Continue - don't block user experience
}
```

## Error Logging

### Context-Rich Logging
```tsx
// ✅ Good - include context
try {
  await processPayment(userId, amount);
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Payment processing failed:', {
    error,
    userId,
    amount,
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
  });
  
  // Send to monitoring service
  captureException(error, {
    tags: { feature: 'payment' },
    extra: { userId, amount },
  });
  
  throw new Error('Payment failed. Please try again.');
}

// ❌ Bad - no context
try {
  await processPayment(userId, amount);
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error);
  throw error;
}
```

### Never Log Sensitive Data
```tsx
// ❌ Bad - logging sensitive data
console.error('Auth failed:', { password, creditCard });

// ✅ Good - sanitized logging
console.error('Auth failed:', { 
  userId,
  timestamp,
  // Never log passwords, tokens, card numbers, etc.
});
```

## Retry Logic

### Smart Retries
```tsx
// ✅ Good - exponential backoff
async function fetchWithRetry(url: string, maxRetries = 3) {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      if (response.ok) return response.json();
      
      // Don't retry client errors (4xx)
      if (response.status >= 400 && response.status < 500) {
        throw new Error('Client error');
      }
      
      // Retry server errors (5xx)
      if (attempt < maxRetries) {
        await new Promise(resolve => 
          setTimeout(resolve, Math.pow(2, attempt) * 1000)
        );
        continue;
      }
      
      throw new Error('Max retries exceeded');
    } catch (error) {
      if (attempt === maxRetries) throw error;
    }
  }
}
```

## Checklist

Before shipping error-prone features:

- [ ] Error boundaries in place
- [ ] All async operations wrapped in try-catch
- [ ] User-friendly error messages (no stack traces)
- [ ] Loading and error states handled
- [ ] Form validation with field-level errors
- [ ] Errors logged with context
- [ ] Fallback UI for failed features
- [ ] Retry logic for network errors
- [ ] Appropriate HTTP status codes
- [ ] No sensitive data in logs
- [ ] Error monitoring configured (Sentry, etc.)
